Ah, student loans—the goblin that hides under your financial bed, grinning as it hoards your interest. See, your monthly payment is like a goblin’s breakfast: it takes the first, greediest bite out of the interest, and only if there’s any crumbs left does it touch the principal.

The way it works is this: each month, your outstanding balance accrues interest based on your annual rate divided by twelve (or daily, if you want to get truly goblin-precise). That interest is the goblin’s toll—it must be paid first. So if your fixed payment is $300, and the interest that month is $250, only $50 actually whacks away at the principal. As the principal shrinks, interest gets smaller—but only after years of the goblin eating first.

So why isn’t it going down as fast as you hoped? Because early on, the principal is fat and juicy, so the interest is a hefty goblin feast. That’s why you see big chunks of your payment vanish without moving the balance—it’s all toll, no treasure. The goblin doesn’t care about your timeline;
